course: history
lesson: 40
title: Australia and New Zealand: a short story
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have lived in Australia for at least 50,000 years, one of the oldest living cultures on Earth, carrying maps of the land in songlines and painting some of the oldest rock art in the world.
explain: In 1788 eleven British ships, the First Fleet, brought convicts and soldiers to Sydney Cove; many convicts had been sent for small crimes like stealing food, and many later became free farmers.
explain: So the same day, 26 January, is Australia Day to some people and Invasion Day to many Aboriginal people, a bit like one event looking very different from two ends of the same street.
explain: Across the sea the Māori had arrived in Aotearoa, New Zealand, from Polynesia about 700 years ago, and in 1840 their chiefs and the British signed the Treaty of Waitangi, whose English and Māori versions said different things.
explain: Both countries have learned to say sorry and keep promises, both remember their Anzac soldiers of 1915 every 25 April, and New Zealand was the first country to give women the vote.
fact: Aboriginal Australians have lived in Australia for at least 50,000 years, with hundreds of languages, and their stories of the Dreaming explain the land, the animals and the law.
fact: Aboriginal rock art is among the oldest in the world, and songlines are songs that carry the map of the land and its stories.
fact: In 1788 the First Fleet of eleven ships brought British convicts and soldiers to Sydney Cove to start a prison colony.
fact: Australia Day on 26 January marks the arrival of the First Fleet, and many Aboriginal people call it Invasion Day.
fact: Over about 80 years Britain sent around 160,000 convicts to Australia, many for small crimes such as stealing food, and many later became free farmers and townspeople.
fact: Gold was found in Australia in the 1850s and people rushed there from all over the world, and in 1901 the colonies joined as the Commonwealth of Australia.
fact: For many years Aboriginal children were taken from their families; they are called the Stolen Generations, and in 2008 the Prime Minister formally apologised.
fact: The Māori arrived in Aotearoa, New Zealand, from Polynesia about 700 years ago and are famous for carving, tattooing, the haka and their navigation.
fact: In 1840 Māori chiefs and the British signed the Treaty of Waitangi; the English and Māori versions said different things, which led to disputes and wars.
fact: New Zealand still works to keep the treaty's promises, and Waitangi Day, 6 February, is its national day.
fact: New Zealand gave women the vote in 1893, before any other self-governing country.
fact: In 1953 Edmund Hillary from New Zealand and Tenzing Norgay from Nepal were the first to climb Mount Everest.
fact: Australian and New Zealand soldiers, the Anzacs, landed at Gallipoli at dawn on 25 April 1915 and are remembered on Anzac Day, 25 April, at dawn services; the soldiers of the two countries fought side by side, and that shared memory helped each country feel like a nation of its own.
exercise: For how long have Aboriginal people lived in Australia? | At least 50,000 years | Only since 1788, when the British came | About 2,000 years | About 500 years
exercise: What are songlines? | Songs that carry the map of the land and its stories | Railway lines in Sydney | Long lines of convicts singing as they marched to work in chains | Radio stations
exercise: What do stories of the Dreaming explain? | The land, the animals and the law | How to build ships and sail them to Britain | The rules of cricket | How to find gold
exercise: How many ships were in the First Fleet? | Eleven | Only three | One hundred | Just two
exercise: When did the First Fleet arrive at Sydney Cove? | 1788 | 1492 | 1901 | 1066
exercise: Why were many convicts sent to Australia? | For small crimes like stealing food | Because they had won a free trip in a prize draw | To find gold in 1788 | To fight the Māori
exercise: About how many convicts did Britain send to Australia? | About 160,000 | About 160 | About 16 million | None at all
exercise: What happened to many convicts later? | They became free farmers and townspeople | They were all shipped back to Britain when their time was up | They became kings | They stayed in prison for ever
exercise: What brought people rushing to Australia in the 1850s? | Gold | Oil fields | Spices and silk | Ice and snow
exercise: When did Australia's colonies join into one nation? | 1901 | 1788 | 1840 | 1967
exercise: Who are the Stolen Generations? | Aboriginal children taken from their families | Convicts who escaped | Gold miners who lost all their gold and had to start again | Soldiers at Gallipoli
exercise: When did the Australian Prime Minister formally say sorry? | 2008 | 1788 | 1901 | 1953
exercise: Where did the Māori come from? | Polynesia | Australia nearby | Great Britain | Southern China
exercise: What is the Māori name for New Zealand? | Aotearoa | Te Waipounamu | Van Diemen's Land | Terra Australis
exercise: Why did the Treaty of Waitangi lead to disputes? | The English and Māori versions said different things | It was never signed by anyone on either side of the meeting | It gave New Zealand to France | It was written only in Latin
exercise: Who first climbed Mount Everest in 1953? | Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay | James Cook and Joseph Banks | Abel Tasman and Tupaia | Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin
exercise: When is Anzac Day? | 25 April | 26 January | 6 February | 11 November
test: aussie mate says 26 jan is just a fun bbq day for everyone. what's he missing? | Many Aboriginal people call it Invasion Day and feel sadness | Nothing, every single person in Australia loves it just the same | It is actually Christmas | It marks the gold rush
test: Two people sign a treaty but their two copies promise different things. What is likely to happen? | Each side expects different things and disputes follow | Everyone agrees for ever, because a signed treaty can never be argued over | The treaty becomes invisible | Nothing, wording never matters
test: Before writing came to Australia, how could a person find their way across hundreds of kilometres? | By following a songline that carries the map of the land | By reading street signs | By using a compass and a paper map brought over from Britain | They could not travel at all
test: Why might a country where people stole bread to survive be a strange start for a nation? | It began as a prison colony, yet convicts became free farmers and citizens | Australia had no laws of any kind, so everybody could do just what they liked | The convicts stayed prisoners for ever | It began as a holiday camp
test: If a government says sorry for taking children from families, why might that matter even years later? | Many of those children and their families are still alive and hurting | It changes nothing at all, because words cannot ever matter after so long | It brings back the gold | It is only a formality
test: Why are Anzac Day services held at dawn? | The Gallipoli landing happened in the early morning | Dawn is the only time when most people are free from work | It is cheaper than evening | It is to see the stars
test: some guy online says hillary climbed everest alone. what's fair? | He climbed it with Tenzing Norgay from Nepal | Yes, completely alone | He flew to the top | Cook climbed it first
test: Some British settlers spoke of Australia as nobody's land. How does very old rock art prove them wrong? | They show people had lived there for a very long time | The art was painted in 1788 | Rock art is made by nature alone | They prove nothing
test: New Zealand gave women the vote in 1893 and Britain in 1918 and 1928. What does that show? | A small country can lead the world on a big idea | Britain was always first | New Zealand was part of the USA | Votes did not matter then
test: A game gives you a free colony in Australia in 1788 with 'no population'. What would a historian change? | Add the Aboriginal peoples who had lived there for over 50,000 years | Nothing, the land was empty | Add Vikings | Add the Romans
test: Why do both Australia and New Zealand count Gallipoli as part of becoming nations? | Their soldiers fought side by side and the memory helped each feel like its own nation | They won the whole war there | They both became British then | They were founded at Gallipoli
exam: Identify the minimum duration for which Aboriginal peoples are known to have inhabited Australia. | Fifty thousand years | Two hundred and fifty years | Five thousand years | Five hundred years
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principal source of dispute arising from the Treaty of Waitangi? | Divergence between its English and Māori texts | Its never having been signed | Its being written in French | Its cession of New Zealand to Australia
exam: Ascertain the year in which the Australian colonies were federated as the Commonwealth of Australia. | Nineteen hundred and one | Seventeen hundred and eighty-eight | Eighteen hundred and forty | Nineteen hundred and fifteen
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the first ascent of Mount Everest. | It was accomplished by Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay in nineteen hundred and fifty-three | It was accomplished by James Cook in seventeen hundred and seventy | It was accomplished by Edmund Hillary alone | It has never been accomplished
